proof.text:
Use [sk-def-sfb-module-pushforward-pullback] for both coefficient rings to write $f^*_{\Lambda'}=\operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha'_f}\circ\operatorname{mapMod}(F)$ and $f^*_{\Lambda}=\operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha_f}\circ\operatorname{mapMod}(F)$. Define the required comparison to be the composite
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Res}_{a_X}\circ f^*_{\Lambda'}
&=\operatorname{Res}_{a_X}\circ\operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha'_f}\circ\operatorname{mapMod}(F)\\
&\xrightarrow{\ \sigma\,\operatorname{mapMod}(F)\ }
\operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha_f}\circ\operatorname{Res}_{Fa_Y}\circ\operatorname{mapMod}(F)\\
&\xrightarrow{\ \operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha_f}\tau^{-1}\ }
\operatorname{Ext}_{\alpha_f}\circ\operatorname{mapMod}(F)\circ\operatorname{Res}_{a_Y}
=f^*_{\Lambda}\circ\operatorname{Res}_{a_Y}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Whiskering the natural isomorphisms $\sigma$ and $\tau^{-1}$ from steps 2 and 1 and composing them gives a natural isomorphism. Its source and target are functors from $\Mod_{\Lambda'_Y}$ to $\Mod_{\Lambda_X}$, as required.
